# tokens.promql — used by tutorial page 3.4 "Tokens and cost"
# Scenario: growing-context (deep dive: the otel_scope_name filter).
# Question: does accumulated context explain token growth?
#
# token.usage records twice per model call, split by gen_ai.token.type
# (input / output). sum is the tokens; count is the model calls. The average
# (sum ÷ count) hides a climb across a session, so page 3.4 reads input-per-call
# over time, not just the average.
#
# POST each block to:
#   monitoring.googleapis.com/v1/projects/$PROJECT_ID/location/global/prometheus/api/v1/query
# with body  query=<urlencoded>  and an ADC bearer token.
#
# Every selector filters on job="adk-metrics-3-4", the OTEL_SERVICE_NAME page
# 3.4 starts its server with, so no other page's run leaks into the reading.
# Change the job to read another server.

# --- Tokens per minute, by type --------------------------------------------
# Two lines, input and output. Input climbs across a growing-context session;
# output stays roughly flat.

sum by ("gen_ai.token.type") (
  rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_sum", job="adk-metrics-3-4"}[10m])
) * 60

# --- Input tokens per model call -------------------------------------------
# sum ÷ count for the input series only. This is the number that rises across
# the session as context accumulates; the deep dive on 3.4 reads it over time.

sum(rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_sum", job="adk-metrics-3-4", "gen_ai.token.type"="input"}[10m]))
  /
sum(rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_count", job="adk-metrics-3-4", "gen_ai.token.type"="input"}[10m]))

# --- Output tokens per model call ------------------------------------------
# The flat counterpart. If this were rising too, the growth would not be
# context alone.

sum(rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_sum", job="adk-metrics-3-4", "gen_ai.token.type"="output"}[10m]))
  /
sum(rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_count", job="adk-metrics-3-4", "gen_ai.token.type"="output"}[10m]))

# --- Tokens per call by type, stepped over time ---------------------------
# Page 3.4 Step 3. Send this to the query_range endpoint (start, end, step=15)
# with a short [1m] window: an instant query returns one averaged number, a
# range query shows input per call climbing while output stays flat.

sum by ("gen_ai.token.type") (rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_sum", job="adk-metrics-3-4"}[1m]))
  /
sum by ("gen_ai.token.type") (rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_count", job="adk-metrics-3-4"}[1m]))

# --- Deep dive: which scope emits which metric -----------------------------
# The metrics arrive under TWO instrumentation scopes, split by family, not one
# (verified live, 2026-09-06):
#   gen_ai.client.*  (token.usage, operation.duration)  ->
#       otel_scope_name="opentelemetry.instrumentation.google_genai"
#   gen_ai.invoke_agent.*, gen_ai.execute_tool.*  (ADK's own meter)  ->
#       otel_scope_name="gcp.vertex.agent"
# So a scope filter must match the metric's own scope. Filtering the client
# token metric to gcp.vertex.agent returns ZERO series — a common mistake. Group
# by the scope to see the split; do not assume one scope owns everything.

sum by (otel_scope_name) (
  rate({"gen_ai.client.token.usage_count", job="adk-metrics-3-4"}[10m])
)
